# PROJECT: AI-POWERED YOGA & WELLNESS PLATFORM

Act as a senior software architect, Go backend engineer, frontend engineer, AI engineer, Firebase specialist, UI/UX designer, and security engineer.

Build a production-minded, responsive, AI-powered yoga business platform. This must be a functional full-stack application, not just a static landing page or UI prototype.

The product should help a yoga studio attract visitors, convert them into customers, manage classes and instructors, accept bookings, manage memberships, and deliver personalized yoga guidance through an AI agent.

## 1. PRODUCT VISION

Create a premium digital yoga experience combining:

- A beautiful public-facing yoga website.
- A real class discovery and booking system.
- User accounts and booking history.
- An AI Yoga Assistant that helps users find suitable classes.
- Instructor profiles and schedules.
- Membership and package management.
- A fully separated administration dashboard.
- Business analytics and operational management.
- A mobile-first, accessible, fast user experience.

The experience should feel calm, premium, intelligent, trustworthy, and easy to use.

Avoid generic template design, cluttered dashboards, excessive gradients, and meaningless AI features.

## 2. NON-NEGOTIABLE ARCHITECTURE

### Backend
Use Go (Golang) as the primary backend language.

Recommended stack:
- Go 1.25+ or the latest stable Go version supported by the deployment environment.
- Gin HTTP framework.
- RESTful JSON API under /api/v1.
- Firebase Admin SDK for Go.
- Firebase Authentication.
- Cloud Firestore as the primary application database.
- Firebase App Check where appropriate.
- Structured logging.
- Request validation.
- Centralized error handling.
- Environment-based configuration.
- Unit and integration tests.

Use a clean, maintainable architecture with clear separation between:
- HTTP handlers/controllers.
- Request and response DTOs.
- Business services.
- Repository interfaces.
- Firestore implementations.
- Authentication and authorization middleware.
- AI provider integrations.
- Background or scheduled tasks where supported.

Keep business logic out of HTTP handlers.

Use Go modules and maintain a clear package structure. Avoid unnecessary microservices. Begin with a modular monolith.

### Frontend
Use TypeScript with React and Vite, or Next.js if server-rendered public pages provide a clear benefit.

Recommended frontend:
- React.
- TypeScript.
- Tailwind CSS.
- shadcn/ui or an equivalent accessible component system.
- Framer Motion or Motion for subtle animations.
- Lucide icons.
- Firebase client SDK for authentication.
- A typed API client that communicates with the Go backend.

The frontend must NOT directly write business-critical booking, payment, membership, or admin data to Firestore.

All business-critical mutations must go through the Go backend, which validates permissions, capacity, ownership, and business rules.

Firebase client SDK access may be used for authentication and explicitly authorized features. Keep Firestore Security Rules restrictive.

### Database
Use Firebase Cloud Firestore as the main database.

Design the system for Firebase's free-tier constraints:
- Minimize unnecessary reads and writes.
- Use pagination and indexed queries.
- Avoid unbounded collection reads.
- Avoid polling when it is not necessary.
- Avoid duplicating large records.
- Use batched writes and transactions where appropriate.
- Never expose service account credentials.
- Keep Firebase billing and quota limits in mind.

Do not assume all Firebase services or hosting products are free under every configuration. Document current quota assumptions and any possible paid-service requirements.

### Important deployment limitation
The Go backend must run as a separate service on a compatible Go hosting platform. Firebase Hosting can serve the frontend, but it does not natively execute a persistent Go server.

Provide a deployment strategy with:
- Frontend hosting.
- Go backend hosting.
- Firebase project configuration.
- Environment variable configuration.
- CORS configuration.
- HTTPS.
- Production and local development environments.

Choose a free-tier-compatible deployment option when available, and document limitations such as sleeping instances, cold starts, usage limits, and changing provider policies.

