- /admin/bookings
- /admin/classes
- /admin/instructors
- /admin/customers
- /admin/memberships
- /admin/ai
- /admin/analytics
- /admin/settings

The public website must NEVER display admin navigation, admin buttons, management links, or admin controls.

The public navigation must not reveal that the business has an admin dashboard.

Use a completely separate admin layout, navigation sidebar, header, and dashboard component tree.

Public users visiting /admin must not gain access to private data.

Protect every admin API endpoint on the Go backend using Firebase ID token verification and server-side role authorization. Hiding a frontend route is NOT sufficient security.

Use Firebase Authentication and an explicit role system:
- CUSTOMER
- INSTRUCTOR
- ADMIN
- SUPER_ADMIN

Only authorized administrators may access administrative functionality.

Never trust a role supplied by the browser. Verify privileges on the server using trusted claims or protected role records.

Admin login must not allow users to grant themselves admin access through signup or editable client profiles.

Support logout, session handling, unauthorized redirects, access-denied states, and secure token verification.

Do not seed a public admin password or embed admin credentials in frontend code.

Provide a secure documented bootstrap procedure for the first administrator.

