## 17. IMPLEMENTATION WORKFLOW

Work in phases and verify each phase before continuing.

Phase 1: Inspect the existing repository and identify its framework, current files, and constraints. Do not overwrite useful existing work.

Phase 2: Write a concise architecture document, project directory tree, data model, API plan, route map, and environment variable list.

Phase 3: Build the visual design system and public landing page.

Phase 4: Implement the Go backend, Firebase initialization, health endpoint, configuration, logging, and error handling.

Phase 5: Implement Firestore repositories, classes, sessions, and public APIs.

Phase 6: Implement Firebase authentication, authorization, user accounts, and protected admin routes.

Phase 7: Implement booking transactions, cancellation rules, and customer booking history.

Phase 8: Build the admin dashboard and management workflows.

Phase 9: Integrate the real AI provider and controlled tool-calling workflow.

Phase 10: Add analytics, tests, security review, responsive refinements, and deployment documentation.

At every phase:
- List the files created or modified.
- Explain the architecture briefly.
- Run relevant checks and tests.
- Fix errors before proceeding.
- Keep documentation up to date.

Do not stop after creating a plan or a static mockup. Implement the application in the current repository.

Do not claim a feature is working unless its implementation and relevant verification support that claim.

If credentials or external service configuration are missing, build the integration boundary, document the exact setup steps, and continue with features that can be tested locally.

Start by inspecting the repository and reporting the proposed architecture and first implementation milestone. Then begin implementation.